'''Southwestern Kaftia''' is a large peninsular region of the continent of [[Kaftia]] on the planet [[Adonia]]. Covering approximately 5,175,957 km², it is home to 14 sovereign states and is one of the most geographically and culturally diverse regions on the planet. The peninsula is bounded by the [[Mesogean Sea]] to the south, the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] to the west and north, and connects to the main body of [[Kaftia]] in the east through the countries of [[Orma]] and [[Ayorin]].

The region is defined by the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], an ancient and heavily eroded chain that runs diagonally from the southwest to the northeast, dividing the peninsula into a hotter, wetter northern basin and a drier, more temperate southern coast.

== Geography ==

=== The Great Kaftian Mountain Range ===
[[File:Great_Kaftian_Mountains_Panorama.jpg|thumb|left|View of the Great Kaftian Mountain Range from the southern foothills in [[Kushish]].]]
The '''[[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]]''' is the dominant topographical feature of Southwestern Kaftia. Running diagonally from the southwest to the northeast, it divides the peninsula roughly into one-third to the south and two-thirds to the north. The range is ancient and heavily eroded, with rounded peaks and wide passes rather than jagged summits.

The mountains are widest and most prominent in the east-central part of the peninsula, particularly where they pass through [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], and eastern [[Burawa]]. The highest point is '''[[Buur Waaq]]''' ("Mountain of Waaq," the sky deity), reaching 2,300 m near the central range. The range gradually subsides as it extends northeast into [[Ayorin]] and southwest toward the coast near [[Fayeha]].

=== Northern Basin ===
The region north of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] comprises approximately two-thirds of the peninsula's total area. It is characterised by broad, flat lowlands dominated by tropical savanna. The terrain slopes gently toward the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] in the north and west.

The most prominent feature of the northern basin is the '''[[Great Kaftian Lake]]''', an enormous freshwater lake occupying a geological depression in eastern [[Galwa]]. With a surface area of approximately 107,000 km², it is one of the largest lakes on [[Adonia]]. The depression extends northwest, with the lake draining toward the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The lake's presence significantly moderates the local climate.

The far northeast of the peninsula, where it connects to main [[Kaftia]], features denser tropical rainforest, particularly in [[Ayorin]] and eastern [[Dakare]].

The southwest of the northern basin is bounded by the '''[[Kurinka Hills]]''', a low ridge (600–1,000 m) that separates [[Fayeha]] from the open savanna to the north and east. While not as imposing as the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], the Kurinka Hills are sufficiently rugged to have historically isolated Fayeha, contributing to its distinct cultural development.

Countries of the northern basin include [[Galwa]], [[Dakare]], [[West Dakare]], [[Sarfone]], [[Wolonia]], and [[Fayeha]].

=== Southern Coast ===
South of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], the terrain is more elevated and varied. The highlands recede within approximately 150–200 km of the coast, giving way to a narrower coastal strip.

* The '''Mesogean-facing coast''' (south-central) features a Mediterranean climate with relatively flat terrain and sporadic hills. This area includes much of southern [[Burawa]].
* The '''Ocean-facing coast''' (southwest) has rougher, more rolling hills leading up to the highland interior. This area includes [[Kijura]].

The southern region contains several major river valleys that drain from the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] southward into the [[Mesogean Sea]]. It is also home to the '''[[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]]''', a lake system shared between [[Sabia]], [[Burawa]], and [[Maianka]] with an area of approximately 8,850 km² that drains south into the [[Mesogean Sea]].

Countries of the southern coast include [[Burawa]] (which also extends north of the range), [[Kijura]], [[Kwalib]], [[Kushish]], [[Maianka]], and [[Sabia]].

=== Eastern Highlands ===
The eastern portion of the peninsula, where it connects to main [[Kaftia]], is marked by elevated terrain. The countries of [[Orma]] and [[Ayorin]] occupy this transitional zone, with [[Orma]] situated in the highlands and [[Ayorin]] straddling the boundary between the peninsula and the broader continent.

== Hydrography ==

=== Great Kaftian Lake ===
{{main|Great Kaftian Lake}}
[[File:Great_Kaftian_Lake_Shore.jpg|thumb|right|The [[Great Kaftian Lake]] facing the Great Kaftian Mountain range near [[Southern National Preserve (West Dakare)]] in [[West Dakare]].]]
The '''[[Great Kaftian Lake]]''' is located in eastern [[Galwa]] at approximately 14°N, 5°W. With a surface area of 107,000 km², it sits in a large geological depression that extends northwest toward the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The lake is a major influence on the regional climate and serves as a critical resource for the surrounding nations.

=== Lake Haro System ===
{{main|Lake Haro}}
The '''[[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]]''' is located in [[Burawa]], [[Maianka]], and [[Sabia]] at approximately 7°N, 8°W. Covering 8,850 km², it is a complex of interconnected lakes close to the southern coast. The system drains southeast into the [[Mesogean Sea]].

=== Major Rivers ===
Southwestern Kaftia has a '''mixed drainage system''', with rivers flowing both northward into the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and the [[Aethiopean Ocean]], and southward into the [[Mesogean Sea]]. The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] serves as the primary watershed divide. River names reflect the linguistic diversity of the region.

