== Geology ==
The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] is the product of an ancient continental collision estimated to have occurred approximately 350–400 million years ago, when two protocontinental masses converged along what is now the diagonal axis of the peninsula. The resulting orogen was once significantly taller, but hundreds of millions of years of erosion have reduced it to its present rounded, subdued profile, with a maximum elevation of 2,300 m at [[Buur Waaq]].

The '''[[Great Kaftian Lake]]''' depression is believed to have formed through a combination of tectonic subsidence and long-term erosion. The basin sits along a failed rift zone where the crust thinned but never fully separated, creating a broad, shallow depression that gradually filled with water. The lake's outlet channel to the northwest follows the path of the original rift.

The southern river valleys contain deep alluvial deposits washed down from the mountains over millions of years, producing some of the most fertile agricultural land in the region. The [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] occupies a series of smaller tectonic depressions near the southeastern coast.

== Coastline and Islands ==
The peninsula has an extensive coastline along both the [[Mesogean Sea]] and the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The southern (Mesogean) coast is generally smoother and lower, while the northern and western (Aethiopean) coast is more irregular, with several offshore island groups.

=== Njangu Jamm ===
The '''[[Njangu Jamm]]''' (Sarafoni: "Islands of Bird Droppings"; Burawi: ''Jasiiradaha Dakare'') are an archipelago off the northern coast of [[Dakare]], named for the vast quantities of {{W|guano}} deposited by seabird colonies. Historically inhabited, the islands were severely depopulated during the slave trade era and continued to lose population as residents migrated to more industrialised areas in the south during the 19th and 20th centuries. Today the islands are sparsely populated, though the guano deposits were historically significant as a fertiliser resource, and recent conservation and tourism efforts have drawn renewed interest.

=== Sarfone Islands ===
Two large islands lie off the northern coast of [[Sarfone]]. These islands are more densely settled than the Njangu Jamm and serve as important fishing and trading ports along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast.

== Climate ==
[[File:Southwestern_Kaftia_Climate_Zones.png|thumb|right|Climate zones of Southwestern Kaftia: {{legend|#228B22|Tropical Savanna}} {{legend|#006400|Rainforest}} {{legend|#FFA500|Mediterranean}} {{legend|#8B4513|Highland}}]]
The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] acts as the primary climatic divide of Southwestern Kaftia. The absence of any major desert on the subcontinent means that the northern basin remains significantly wetter than comparable latitudes on other landmasses, as there is no arid interior to suppress moisture flow.

=== Northern Basin ===
The region north of the mountains experiences a '''{{W|tropical savanna climate}}''', characterised by high temperatures, high humidity, and substantial rainfall.

* '''Temperature''': Average 24–30°C year-round, with little seasonal variation. Slightly cooler near the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] due to lake-effect moderation (the lake cools daytime air by 2–4°C and warms nighttime air).
* '''Rainfall''': 1,500–2,500 mm annually. A distinct wet season runs from approximately April to October, driven by moist ocean air from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The dry season (November–March) is milder than comparable tropical regions due to the absence of desert influence, with intermittent rainfall continuing.
* '''Humidity''': Consistently high (65–85%), particularly near the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and the northern coast.

=== Northeastern Rainforest Zone ===
The far northeast, where the peninsula connects to main [[Kaftia]], receives the heaviest rainfall in the region (2,500–3,500 mm annually), supporting dense tropical rainforest in [[Ayorin]] and eastern [[Dakare]]. Temperatures are slightly cooler (23–28°C) due to persistent cloud cover and elevation.

=== Southern Coast ===
South of the mountains, the climate transitions to a '''{{W|Mediterranean climate}}''' along the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast, characterised by warm dry summers and mild wet winters.

* '''Temperature''': Summer averages of 25–32°C; winter averages of 12–20°C. The most temperate conditions are found near the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] in [[Sabia]], where lake-moderated breezes and coastal proximity create mild, stable conditions year-round (18–26°C).
* '''Rainfall''': 500–900 mm annually, concentrated in the winter months (October–March). The river valleys receive additional moisture from orographic rainfall off the mountains, supporting fertile agriculture.
* '''Ocean-facing southwest coast''' ([[Fayeha]], [[Kijura]]): Somewhat wetter (700–1,100 mm) with rougher terrain and more rolling hills, receiving moisture from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] as well.

=== Highlands ===
The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] itself experiences a '''{{W|subtropical highland climate}}''', with cooler temperatures (15–22°C average), frequent mist and cloud cover, and moderate rainfall (1,000–1,500 mm). [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], and highland [[Orma]] all experience notably cooler conditions than the surrounding lowlands.

== Ecology ==
The climatic and topographic diversity of Southwestern Kaftia supports several distinct ecoregions.

=== Great Kaftian Savanna ===
The dominant biome of the northern basin, the '''[[Great Kaftian Savanna]]''' is a vast tropical grassland stretching across [[Dakare]], [[West Dakare]], [[Galwa]], and [[Sarfone]]. It is characterised by tall grasses interspersed with scattered trees, and supports large populations of grazing and browsing wildlife.

=== Dakare-Galwa Wetlands ===
The lowlands surrounding the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and its drainage channels form the '''[[Dakare-Galwa Wetlands]]''', an extensive system of marshes, floodplains, and seasonal lakes. The wetlands are among the most biodiverse areas in the region, supporting vast populations of waterbirds, fish, and amphibians.

=== Ayorin Rainforest ===
The '''[[Ayorin Rainforest]]''' covers the far northeast of the peninsula, primarily in [[Ayorin]] and eastern [[Dakare]]. It is the westernmost extension of the broader Kaftian equatorial rainforest belt, featuring dense canopy cover, high species diversity, and significant rainfall.

=== Kaftian Montane Forest ===
The slopes of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] support '''[[Kaftian Montane Forest]]''', a cool, misty woodland found at elevations above approximately 800 m. These forests are home to species adapted to cooler temperatures and higher moisture, and serve as important watershed areas for the rivers draining both north and south.

=== Burawi Maquis ===
The southern coastal strip along the [[Mesogean Sea]] supports '''[[Burawi Maquis]]''', a {{W|maquis shrubland}} of drought-adapted shrubs, aromatic herbs, and small evergreen trees. This biome is best developed in southern [[Burawa]], [[Kijura]], and coastal [[Sabia]].

=== Haro Lakeland ===
The '''[[Haro Lakeland]]''' ecoregion surrounds the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] in [[Sabia]] and [[Maianka]]. Fed by both lake moisture and orographic rainfall from the nearby highlands, it supports a unique mosaic of wetland, grassland, and Mediterranean woodland, with the most temperate climate in the region.

== Fauna ==
The diverse biomes of Southwestern Kaftia support a rich variety of animal life, ranging from the large megafauna of the northern savanna to the unique endemic species of the southern highlands.

=== Mammals ===
The [[Great Kaftian Savanna]] supports one of the largest concentrations of large mammals on the continent. Herbivore populations include vast herds of '''{{W|African buffalo|Kaftian buffalo}}''', '''{{W|Plains zebra|savanna zebra}}''', and numerous antelope species such as the '''{{W|Common eland|giant eland}}''' and '''{{W|Impala|black-faced impala}}'''. The '''{{W|African bush elephant|Kaftian elephant}}''', slightly smaller than its mainland counterparts, migrates seasonally between the open savanna and the woodland fringes. A distinct subspecies known as the '''{{W|African forest elephant|Mesogean pygmy elephant}}''' historically inhabited the coastal maquis of the south, having migrated from regions near [[Desket]] in Eastern Kaftia, though it is now extinct.

Apex predators include the '''{{W|Lion|Kaftian lion}}''', noted for the darker manes found in the highland populations, the '''{{W|Leopard|savanna leopard}}''', and packs of '''{{W|African wild dog|painted hunting dog}}'''. The [[Ayorin Rainforest]] in the northeast is a biodiversity hotspot for primates, home to populations of '''{{W|Chimpanzee|Ayorin chimpanzee}}''' and several species of '''{{W|Colobus|colobus monkey}}'''. In the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], the elusive '''{{W|Leopard|mountain leopard}}''' preys on the sure-footed '''{{W|Nubian ibex|Kaftian ibex}}'''.

=== Avifauna ===
Southwestern Kaftia lies on a major migratory flyway, hosting millions of birds annually.
* '''Wetlands''': The [[Dakare-Galwa Wetlands]] and the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] support large breeding colonies of '''{{W|Greater flamingo|greater flamingo}}''', '''{{W|Great white pelican|white pelican}}''', and the '''{{W|Saddle-billed stork|saddle-billed stork}}'''.
* '''Seabirds''': The [[Njangu Jamm]] archipelago is famous for its massive colonies of guano-producing seabirds, primarily the '''{{W|Cape cormorant|Kaftian cormorant}}''' and the '''{{W|Masked booby|masked booby}}'''.
* '''Raptors''': The mountains are home to significant populations of '''{{W|Verreaux's eagle|black eagle}}''' and the scavenging '''{{W|Bearded vulture|bearded vulture}}'''.

=== Aquatic Life ===
The [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and the major river systems are inhabited by large populations of '''{{W|Hippopotamus|river hippopotamus}}''' and the formidable '''{{W|Nile crocodile|Kaftian crocodile}}'''. The lake itself contains hundreds of endemic {{W|cichlid}} fish species, which are a vital food source for the local human population. The coastal waters of the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and [[Mesogean Sea]] are rich in marine life, supporting productive fisheries of '''{{W|Tuna|yellowfin tuna}}''', '''{{W|Mackerel|king mackerel}}''', and '''{{W|Sardine|sardines}}'''.

== Demographics ==
Southwestern Kaftia has an estimated total population of approximately '''350 million''' (2026 estimate), making it one of the most densely populated regions of [[Kaftia]]. The majority of the population is concentrated in the southern coastal areas and the Great Kaftian Lake basin.

=== Ethnic and Linguistic Groups ===
The peninsula is home to four major language families:

* '''[[Burawi language|Burawi]]''': The most widely spoken language in the region, dominant in [[Burawa]], [[Fayeha]], [[Kijura]], and [[Galwa]]. The historical expansion of the [[Burawa|Burawi Empire]] spread the language across much of the peninsula.
* '''[[Dakari language|Dakari]]''': Spoken primarily in [[Dakare]] and [[West Dakare]].
* '''[[Sarafoni language|Sarafoni]]''': Spoken in [[Sarfone]], [[Wolonia]], and parts of northern [[Galwa]]. It is also the native language of the [[Njangu Jamm]] islanders.
* '''[[Orma languages|Orma]]''': Spoken in [[Orma]], [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], and parts of [[Sabia]] and [[Maianka]].

