== Natural Resources ==

=== Agriculture ===
The varied climate of Southwestern Kaftia supports a wide range of agricultural production. Crops are broadly divided into those indigenous to the region and those introduced through {{W|Mesogean Sea|Mesogean}} and [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] contact.

==== Indigenous Crops (Northern Basin) ====
The tropical savanna and rainforest zones of the northern basin gave rise to a distinct set of domesticated crops:
* '''{{W|Sorghum}}''' and '''{{W|pearl millet}}''': The oldest cultivated grains of the region, domesticated in the northern savanna zones ([[Dakare]], [[Galwa]]) by approximately 5,000 BCE. They remain staple crops in the northern basin.
* '''{{W|Yam (vegetable)|Yams}}''': Domesticated in the wetter forest-savanna transition zones, particularly in [[Ayorin]] and eastern [[Dakare]].
* '''{{W|Cowpea}}''': An indigenous {{W|legume}}, widely cultivated across the northern and central regions.
* '''{{W|Oil palm}}''': Native to the wetter northern lowlands. Palm oil is a major export commodity.
* '''{{W|Shea tree|Shea}}''': Found in the savanna zones, producing oil used for food and cosmetics.

==== Indigenous Crops (Southern Coast) ====
The drier, temperate [[Burawi Maquis]] and highland zones of the south supported a separate set of native flora, adapted to the seasonal {{W|Mediterranean climate}} of warm dry summers and mild wet winters:
* '''{{W|Common fig|Wild fig}}''': Native to the southern coastal hills and river valleys, harvested long before Mesogean contact.
* '''{{W|Carob}}''': A drought-resistant {{W|legume|leguminous}} tree native to the [[Burawi Maquis]], producing sweet pods used as food and animal fodder.
* '''Native grains''': Wild varieties of {{W|emmer}} and other grasses were gathered and eventually cultivated in the temperate river valleys, predating the introduction of cultivated {{W|wheat}} and {{W|barley}} by Mesogean traders.
* '''Root tubers''': Several species of drought-adapted tubers, dormant through the dry summer and sprouting in the wet winter, were a staple of pre-Mesogean southern agriculture.
* '''Aromatic herbs''': The [[Burawi Maquis]] supports native populations of {{W|thyme}}, {{W|oregano}}, and related aromatic species, gathered wild and later cultivated for culinary and medicinal use.
* '''Native pulses''': {{W|Lentil}}-like legumes adapted to the temperate climate were cultivated in the southern highlands and coastal plains.

==== Introduced Crops ====
* '''{{W|Olive}}''' and '''{{W|Grape vine|Grape}}''': Introduced to the southern coast through Mesogean trade, now cultivated extensively in the {{W|Mediterranean climate}} zones of southern [[Burawa]], [[Kijura]], and [[Sabia]]. Olive oil and wine are significant products of the [[Burawi Maquis]] zone.
* '''{{W|Wheat}}''' and '''{{W|Barley}}''': Introduced via Mesogean contact, cultivated in the temperate southern river valleys.
* '''{{W|Citrus}}''': Introduced later through Volisanian trade networks, now grown along the southern coast and in the [[Haro Lakeland]].

=== Minerals ===
The ancient rocks of the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] contain exposed mineral veins deposited during the original {{W|orogeny}}. Significant deposits include:
* '''{{W|Iron ore}}''', '''{{W|copper}}''', and '''{{W|tin}}''': Found throughout the central highlands, particularly in [[Kushish]], [[Kwalib]], and highland [[Orma]]. Iron ore was the basis of early metallurgy in the region.

The peninsula is notably lacking in {{W|gold}} and {{W|silver}} deposits. Historically, this scarcity was a major driver of [[Mesogean Sea]] trade: Mesogean merchants exchanged gold and silver for the region's abundant iron, copper, and other goods. The flow of precious metals into Southwestern Kaftia through trade had a lasting influence on the development of coastal economies and the rise of the city-states.

=== Hydrocarbons ===
The southern [[Burawa|Burawi]] coast along the [[Mesogean Sea]] possesses some of the largest known reserves of '''{{W|petroleum}}''' and '''{{W|natural gas}}''' in Southwestern Kaftia. These reserves, located in offshore and near-shore formations, are a major component of [[Burawa]]'s modern economy.

=== Fisheries ===
Both the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] and the [[Lake Haro|Lake Haro System]] support extensive freshwater fisheries. The [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and [[Mesogean Sea]] coasts provide access to productive marine fisheries, with the [[Sarfone Islands]] and [[Njangu Jamm]] serving as important bases for the fishing industry.

=== Guano ===
The [[Njangu Jamm]] archipelago off the coast of [[Dakare]] historically supported large seabird colonies, producing substantial deposits of {{W|guano}}. These deposits were exploited as a natural {{W|fertiliser}} and were a significant resource during the pre-industrial period.

== Seismology and Natural Hazards ==
Southwestern Kaftia is a '''geologically stable''' region. The failed {{W|rift zone}} underlying the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] depression has been dormant for millions of years, and the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] is a product of an ancient {{W|orogeny}} with no active {{W|tectonic}} processes. {{W|Earthquake}}s are exceedingly rare and typically of very low magnitude.

The primary natural hazards in the region are '''{{W|hydrological}}''' in nature:
* '''Seasonal flooding''' affects the lowlands of the northern basin, particularly along the [[Great Kaftian Lake]] drainage and the [[Dakare-Galwa Wetlands]], during the wet season (April–October).
* '''{{W|Tropical cyclone|Tropical storms}}''' occasionally make landfall along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast, primarily affecting [[Sarfone]], [[Wolonia]], and northern [[Galwa]].
* '''{{W|Drought}}''' is an intermittent risk in the southern Mediterranean zones, particularly during extended dry summers, although the region's overall moisture supply mitigates severe drought.

== History ==
{{main|Prehistoric Southwestern Kaftia|Ancient Southwestern Kaftia}}
Archaeological evidence indicates human presence in Southwestern Kaftia dating back over 300,000 years. The peninsula was populated through two distinct migration corridors: a '''northeastern route''' through the [[Ayorin Rainforest]] corridor (ancestors of the [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]] and [[Dakari people|Dakari]] peoples, settling north of the mountains) and an '''eastern highland route''' through [[Orma]] (ancestors of the [[Burawi people|Burawi]] and [[Orma people|Orma]] peoples, settling south of the mountains). The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] divided these two streams, laying the foundations for the region's deep linguistic and ethnic divisions.

Organised agriculture developed by approximately 5,000 BCE, arising from a combination of independent innovation and diffusion from main [[Kaftia]]. During the {{W|Iron Age}}, the southern region gave rise to the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] (controlling the [[Buran River|Buran]] valley and [[Lake Haro]]), the [[Kingdom of Sago|Kingdom of Sago]] (dominating the western [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys), and a network of coastal city-states — most notably [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] — engaged in trade across the [[Mesogean Sea]] with the maritime civilisations of the southern shore. The [[Mesogean language|Mesogean]] vocabulary layer in several regional languages dates to this period.

In 403 AD, the [[Mazar-Iskhal alliance]] united city-states along the [[Buran River|Buran]] and [[Dafar River|Dafar]] rivers, forming the first [[Burawa|Burawi kingdom]]. By the 16th century, this kingdom had expanded through conquest into a vast feudal empire. A prolonged civil conflict known as the [[Long Decades War]] subsequently split the empire into the [[Western Burawi Empire]] and the [[Eastern Burawi Empire]], divided along the [[Dimar River|Dimar]], the [[Great Kaftian Lake]], and the [[Kitale River|Kitale]].

The modern political landscape of the peninsula emerged through a complex process of imperial decline, republican revolution (the [[Samara Declaration]] of 1832), socialist transformation under [[Maana Kadar]] in the 1930s, and the upheaval of the [[Great Adonian War]], which saw the invasion of [[Burawa]] by [[Volisania]]. Today, Southwestern Kaftia comprises 14 sovereign states.

